>
  <p>Falling behind feels like a cliff. The pile grows, you avoid it because facing it feels awful, and avoiding it makes the pile grow. Breaking that loop does not take a burst of willpower. It takes a plan calm enough to follow when you feel like hiding.</p>

  <h2 id="audit">Audit what is due</h2>
  <p>Write down everything outstanding in one place: assignments, readings, topics missed, upcoming exams. It will feel worse for about ten minutes and better after that, because a known list is smaller than a vague dread. Sirois and Pychyl's work shows avoidance is mostly about escaping bad feelings in the moment; naming the list removes much of the thing you were avoiding.</p>

  <h2 id="triage">Triage the list</h2>
  <p>You probably cannot do all of it well, and trying to is what keeps you frozen. Sort each item: high value and due soon (do first), high value and later (schedule), low value (minimise, submit late on purpose, or drop). Use the <a href="/tools/gpa-calculator/">GPA calculator</a> to see which pieces actually move your grade so you spend effort where it pays.</p>

  <h2 id="momentum">Rebuild momentum</h2>
  <p>Do not open with an all-nighter; you will crash. Pick one small, finishable task and complete it today to prove the situation is movable. Then break the bigger items into dated steps with the <a href="/tools/assignment-planner/">assignment planner</a>, and spread the catch-up across days with the <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> rather than one brutal session. Specific, achievable targets (Locke and Latham) beat "catch up on everything".</p>

  <h2 id="prevent">Stop it recurring</h2>
  <p>Once you are level, a weekly review is what keeps you there: ten minutes checking the next two weeks and booking the work in. Put exam dates in the <a href="/tools/exam-countdown/">exam countdown</a> so revision starts on time. For the habit side, read <a href="/guides/how-to-stop-procrastinating/">how to stop procrastinating</a> and <a href="/guides/time-management-for-students/">time management for students</a>.</p>
</Guide>
